drm, scene: keep color-pipeline KMS state stable between frames

With the final color conversion offloaded to a KMS color pipeline, every
commit created new DATA property blobs for the matrix and LUT colorops
and destroyed them afterwards. KMS compares blob IDs, so each flip
programmed a "new" color pipeline even when its contents were
unchanged. amdgpu treats that as a color-management update and performs
full display-core validation in atomic check for every flip, and for
every TEST_ONLY probe before it.

The scene also re-tested the offload on every frame once it had been
accepted, because a successful test reset the retry counter to zero. That
TEST_ONLY commit runs inside wlr_scene_output_build_state(), i.e. in the
frame path. On a 4K 160 Hz HDR output it took 7.8 ms median (12.8 ms p95)
against a 6.25 ms refresh interval, so frames missed vblank and a 90 fps
game was displayed at roughly 45 or 22 fps. With the offload disabled the
same build took 0.012 ms.

Keep one DATA blob per colorop together with a copy of its contents, and
reuse the blob ID while the contents are byte-identical. The colorop owns
the blob: replacement or plane teardown destroys our handle, and KMS keeps
its own reference while the blob is committed. Unchanged recipes are now
unchanged KMS state.

In the scene, remember the recipe/swapchain key accepted by the last
successful test and skip further TEST_ONLY commits for frames that change
only buffer contents, damage, timelines, scale, transform or subpixel.
Any other committed state, a recipe or swapchain change, an image
description or mode change, or a disallowed direct scanout (which is
how a rejected real commit's fallback render is built) clears the cache
and tests again.

